			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4455698">
									

<p>My understanding was that the O5 got removed by the other O5s because he did something very bad, and Jimmy was just the one who told them what he'd done. The awkward thing about Jimmy is that his arguments seem to be always *justified*. It's hard to publicly call for the head of somebody who is known to only go after people who are doing something illegal! At least, on those grounds. His knowledge of all the secret files is still a massive security risk, of course.</p>
<p>Not sure that it's about the lower-ranking researchers being *lazy*, more that they're deliberately shielding him because they consider him to be on their side and think the O5s only have it in for him because he spoiled their nasty schemes.</p>
<p>It's still true that that letter at the end is awkward - the idea is that site directors shouldn't have access to it, but in such a context, any director who saw that there was a note on the file that they didn't have access to would immediately want to know what it was and hunt around until they found a low-level researcher who was prepared to spill the beans! I don't know what another reasonably succint way of expressing this would be. Hopefully the author can think up something.</p>

							</div>

								
					
